<h1>Minimum-Pressure Accumulation Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Minimum-pressure accumulation lets products lightly touch while they queue. Drive pressure is set just high enough to move the heaviest product, so the rollers slip under stopped product and backpressure stays low. <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/accumulation/minimum-pressure-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hytrol</a> states its minimum-pressure conveyors can be adjusted to bring backpressure to 2% of the live load. It costs less than <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/zero-pressure/">zero-pressure accumulation (ZPA)</a> and packs more product per foot, which makes it a good fit for durable products like sealed corrugated cases and rigid totes.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Minimum-Pressure Works</h2>
<p>The common design is a belt-driven live roller conveyor with adjustable drive pressure. In Hytrol's 190-ACC, suspension angles support pressure rollers that hold the drive V-belt against the tread rollers, and knurled nuts set how hard the belt presses. The drive runs continuously. When the lead product stops, the rollers under the queued products slip against the belt, so each product pushes on the next with only light force. When the lead product is released, the queue moves forward together.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/minpressure-acsi-190cap.webp" width="1000" height="785" alt="Straight roller conveyor with green side frames on floor supports, closely spaced steel rollers, a drive belt visible under the rollers at the near end, and a motor with a yellow guard under the bed." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A belt-driven live roller conveyor built for minimum-pressure accumulation: the Model 190CAP from Automated Conveyor Systems. The belt under the rollers drives them, and items touch while they wait, which is the defining difference from zero-pressure accumulation. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.automatedconveyors.com/catalog/zone-accumulating-min-pressure.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Automated Conveyor Systems</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Setting Drive Pressure</h3>
<p>Hytrol's <a href="https://cdn.hytrol.com/2015_682_190acc.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">190-ACC manual</a> sets pressure with the conveyor running:</p>
<ol>
<li>Reduce pressure on all tread rollers to zero by loosening the adjustment nuts.</li>
<li>Place the heaviest item to be conveyed on the infeed end, and tighten the nuts under it only enough to move it slightly.</li>
<li>Continue adjusting ahead of the item until it travels the full length.</li>
<li>Return the item to the infeed end and confirm it travels the full length. Repeat in any problem area.</li>
</ol>
<p>On curves and spurs (Hytrol's LRC, LRS, and LRSS), snub sheaves hold the V-belt against the rollers, and the same rule applies: apply only enough force to move the heaviest item.</p>
<h3>Key Characteristics</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Backpressure:</strong> light and adjustable. Hytrol cites adjustment down to 2% of the live load.</li>
<li><strong>Zones and sensors:</strong> the mechanical design described in the 190-ACC manual has no zone controllers or compressed air. Pressure is set mechanically along the conveyor.</li>
<li><strong>Force on the lead product:</strong> because backpressure is a share of the live load, the force on the lead product grows with the number and weight of products queued behind it.</li>
<li><strong>Release mode:</strong> the queue advances together when the lead product is released.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Other Drive Technologies</h3>
<p><strong>Line-shaft</strong> conveyors give light-pressure accumulation through O-ring slip: the spools slip on the rotating shaft when product is held. Products still touch, and backpressure depends on O-ring condition and shaft cleanliness.</p>
<p><strong>Chain-driven live roller</strong> cannot slip, so a continuous chain section accumulates with full backpressure. Use zoned chain-driven conveyors for pallet accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>MDR (Motor Driven Roller)</strong> conveyors are built around zone-by-zone control, so they are normally used for zero-pressure accumulation rather than minimum-pressure.</p>
<h2 id="comparison-heading">Minimum-Pressure vs. Zero-Pressure</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Factor</th>
<th>Minimum-Pressure</th>
<th>Zero-Pressure (ZPA)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Product contact</td>
<td>Light contact, adjustable backpressure</td>
<td>No contact (gaps maintained)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>How drive is limited</td>
<td>Drive pressure set low, rollers slip</td>
<td>Drive removed or stopped zone by zone</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sensors and zone controls</td>
<td>Few or none</td>
<td>Sensor and controller in every zone</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Compressed air</td>
<td>Not needed for mechanical designs</td>
<td>Needed for pneumatic zone designs, not for MDR or zoned chain drive</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Accumulation density</td>
<td>Higher (products touch)</td>
<td>Lower (gaps between products)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Release mode</td>
<td>Queue advances together</td>
<td>Singulated or slug</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Main cost drivers</td>
<td>Conveyor length, pressure adjustment hardware</td>
<td>Sensor, controller, and drive or brake in every zone</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Best for</td>
<td>Durable cases, totes, rigid containers</td>
<td>Fragile, labeled, premium products</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 id="when-heading">When to Specify Minimum-Pressure</h2>
<h3>Good Applications</h3>
<ul>
<li>Sealed corrugated cases (RSC, HSC, display cases)</li>
<li>Rigid plastic totes and containers</li>
<li>Heavy, dense, stable products</li>
<li>Products with robust labels (not pressure-sensitive)</li>
<li>Distribution center case handling</li>
<li>Manufacturing line work-in-process buffer</li>
</ul>
<h3>Poor Applications (Use ZPA Instead)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Glass containers (breakage risk)</li>
<li>Pressure-sensitive labels (scuffing)</li>
<li>Shrink-wrapped products (deformation)</li>
<li>Open or unsealed containers</li>
<li>Premium packaging (visible damage concerns)</li>
<li>Lightweight or tall products that may tip under backpressure</li>
<li>Pallets on chain-driven conveyor (chain drive cannot slip, so use zoned accumulation)</li>
</ul>
<h4>Quick Decision Test</h4>
<p>Load a full-length queue of your heaviest product and hold the lead product. Does the lead product, or any product in the queue, crush, scuff, shift, or tip?</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No:</strong> Minimum-pressure is suitable (lower cost)</li>
<li><strong>Yes:</strong> Specify <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/zero-pressure/">zero-pressure (ZPA)</a></li>
</ul>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Backpressure Setting</h3>
<p>Set pressure for the heaviest product and no higher. Extra pressure raises the force on the lead product with no benefit. Recheck the setting when the product mix changes, and inspect pressure rollers and belt during routine maintenance, since wear changes the drive force.</p>
<h3>Queue Length</h3>
<p>Because backpressure scales with the load queued behind the lead product, a long run of heavy product can push harder on the lead product than a short one. Where the queue is long, the products are heavy, or the downstream machine is sensitive to infeed pressure, break the run with a stop, a zero-pressure section, or a metering device ahead of the machine.</p>
<h3>Product Stability</h3>
<p>Minimum-pressure works best with stable products that resist tipping under pressure from behind. Tall, narrow products may tip when contacted by upstream products. Wide, low products are ideal. For marginal stability cases, lower the pressure setting, shorten the queue, or add <a href="/components/guide-rails/">guide rails</a> to contain products.</p>
<h3>Utilities</h3>
<p>The mechanical minimum-pressure design described in Hytrol's 190-ACC manual needs electrical power for the drive and no compressed air. Zero-pressure designs that use pneumatic zones need plant air at the pressure the manufacturer specifies. For zero-pressure without compressed air, consider <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR systems</a>.</p>
