<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a minimum-pressure accumulation conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor that lets products lightly touch while they queue. Drive pressure is set just high enough to move the heaviest product, so rollers slip under stopped product and backpressure stays low. Hytrol states its minimum-pressure conveyors can bring backpressure to 2% of the live load. It suits durable products like sealed corrugated cases and rigid totes. See <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/zero-pressure/">zero-pressure systems</a> for no-contact accumulation.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How does minimum-pressure accumulation work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>On a belt-driven design such as Hytrol's 190-ACC, pressure rollers hold the drive belt against the carrying rollers. Pressure is set by reducing it to zero, placing the heaviest product on the conveyor, and increasing it only until the product moves, along the full length. When the lead product stops, rollers under the queue slip. When it is released, the queue moves forward together.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between minimum-pressure and zero-pressure accumulation?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Minimum-pressure lets products touch with light, adjustable backpressure. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/zero-pressure/">Zero-pressure (ZPA)</a> stops each product in its own zone with a gap. Minimum-pressure costs less (few or no sensors and zone controls) and packs more product per foot. ZPA is the choice for fragile, labeled, or premium products.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">When should I use minimum-pressure instead of zero-pressure?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>For sealed corrugated cases, rigid plastic containers, heavy totes, and products with durable labels that tolerate light, continuous contact. Backpressure is a share of the live load, so the force on the lead product grows with the queue behind it. Test with a full queue of your heaviest product. Use ZPA for fragile products, pressure-sensitive labels, or premium appearance requirements.</p>

</div>
</div>
